course: dictionary
lesson: 7071
title: Patrician, a word from Ancient Rome
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: A word can carry a whole history with it. Patrician is one of those words. In history lessons on Ancient Rome, patricians were the noble families, and plebeians were the ordinary people. «Rich old families were patricians and ordinary citizens were plebeians.» The sentence sets the two words side by side, so the contrast gives you the meaning: patricians are the rich, old, noble families at the top.
explain: The sentence gives another clue: «the plebeians went on strike by leaving the city until they won more rights.» The ordinary people were not happy with how little power they had, so they left the city to make a point. That tells you the patricians had more rights and power than the plebeians.
explain: Patrician can also describe a person. A patrician face or voice looks or sounds upper-class, calm, proud and elegant. Test it by swapping in a word you know: a proud, elegant voice fits the sentence, so your meaning is probably right.
fact: Patrician is a noun (a patrician, patricians) and an adjective (patrician).
fact: «Patrician» means a member of an old, rich, noble family in Ancient Rome.
fact: Patrician can also mean a rich, upper-class person today, or describe someone who is proud and elegant in manner.
fact: Plebeians were the ordinary people in Ancient Rome, while patricians were the noble families.
fact: At first, only patricians could hold top jobs.
exercise: «Rich old families were patricians and ordinary citizens were plebeians.» Which group were the rich, noble families? | Ordinary citizens | *Patricians | Plebeians | Strikers
exercise: «The plebeians went on strike by leaving the city until they won more rights.» What does plebeians mean here? | The rich noble families | The leaders of the city | Visitors from far away | *The ordinary people
exercise: «A patrician face looked calm and proud.» What does patrician suggest about the face? | Dirty and tired | *Upper-class, dignified and elegant | Sleepy and cold | Young and playful
test: «Her patrician manner made everyone sit up straight.» What does patrician mean here? | *Proud, elegant and upper-class | Very hungry | Lazy and messy | Loud and cheerful
test: «The plebeians went on strike by leaving the city until they won more rights.» Why did the plebeians leave the city? | To have a holiday | *To win more rights | To join the patricians for dinner | To find a bigger house
exam: Which sentence best uses context to work out the meaning of patrician? | *Rich old families were patricians and ordinary citizens were plebeians, so patricians were the noble families | A patrician is a dog that barks loudly | Patricians always lived in the countryside with the animals | Patrician is a word for a long walk
